 "max_tree_depth": 3, "reasoning_ledger": {"anchor": {"hypothesis": "B1", "t": 0, "posterior_at_anchor": 0.584}, "leader": {"branch_id": "B1.1", "label": "Choledocholithiasis (Common Bile Duct Stones)", "posterior": 0.404, "leading_since_t": 0, "n_revisions": 0}, "leader_evidence": {"confirming": 3, "disconfirming": 0, "last_disconfirming_digest": ""}, "action_intents": [{"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "confirm"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}], "considered_alternatives": ["B2", "B5", "B4"]}, "discriminator_hints": "[Knowledge Layer: coverage=60%, source=both]\n\nAcute Cholangitis / Biliary Tract Infection vs Choledocholithiasis (Common Bile Duct Stones):\n  Favours first:  chills, fatigue, itchy skin, pain in the upper right abdomen, pale stools\n  Favours second: 2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal distention, abdominal pain, abnormal astrocyte morphology, abnormal brainstem mri signal intensity\n\nAcute Cholangitis / Biliary Tract Infection vs Mirizzi Syndrome (Gallstone-Induced Biliary Obstruction):\n  Favours first:  chills, dark urine, fatigue, fever, itchy skin\n  Favours second: 2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal distention, abnormal astrocyte morphology, abnormal brainstem mri signal intensity, abnormal epiphyseal ossification\n\nCholedocholithiasis (Common Bile Duct Stones) vs Mirizzi Syndrome (Gallstone-Induced Biliary Obstruction):\n  Favours first:  abdominal pain, cholangitis, clay-colored stools, dark urine, fever\n\nRelated to Choledocholithiasis (Common Bile Duct Stones): bile duct disease, gallstones, genetic biliary tract disease\n\nRelated to Mirizzi Syndrome (Gallstone-Induced Biliary Obstruction): extrahepatic cholestasis\n\n[Indirect reasoning chains (PrimeKG 2-hop):]\n  A 23-year-old woman presents to the emergency department with severe abdominal pain → abdominal distention → Choledocholithiasis (Common Bile Duct Stones)\n  A 23-year-old woman